accession number   removed_accession_id  report status  final  type  surgical pathology  pathology report  removed_accession_id  breast   excisional tissue w margins  accessioned on  removed_date  lester  susan  m d  ph d   diagnosis  by lester susan m d  ph d   left breast  subareolar  wire localized excision for a mass   subareolar sclerosing papillary duct hyperplasia with atypia  1 5cm  with  loss of myoepithelial cells around the periphery  see note    the lesion is   0 1cm from the nearest margin  not oriented    stromal changes consistent with the prior needle biopsy   immunoperoxidase studies are performed with the following results   p63  removed_accession_id   removed_accession_id  preservation of myoepithelial cells in  the center of the lesion with loss  around the periphery  smooth muscle alpha actin same as  removed_accession_id   calponin same as  removed_accession_id   estrogen receptor positive in center  weaker in  periphery  progesterone receptor same as er   removed_accession_id  neu negative  0   34beta removed_accession_id  positive  diffuse  neuron specific enolase positive   weak  chromogranin negative  synaptophysin negative  note  an unusual feature of this lesion is the loss of myoepithelial  cells in small nests of epithelial cells around the periphery of the  lesion  some of these nests of cells appear to infiltrate around small  ducts  in some areas  these nests are associated with stromal changes and  suggest epithelial displacement due to the prior needle biopsy  other  areas are not associated with such stromal changes and raised the  possibility of invasion  however  a pattern of multifocal invasion  associated with a benign lesion would be highly unusual   immunoperoxidase studies show strong immunoreactivity for cytokeratin  34beta removed_accession_id  and weak or absent immunoreactivity for neuroendocrine markers   this pattern is typical for hyperplasia and would be not be characteristic  for an unusual spindle cell carcinoma in situ   the case was sent to dr  p p  rosen who performed additional  immunoperoxidase studies  see his complete report below   he states that  basement membrane is retained throughout the lesion  the cytologic  appearance of the cells at the periphery is similar to that in the center  of the lesion and he concludes that the lesion is not invasive carcinoma  and he interprets the lesion as  subareolar sclerosing papillary duct  hyperplasia with atypia    consultation report from dr  p p  rosen  new york   presbyterian hospital  new  york  ny   removed_accession_id   6 19 03    these slides show a complex sclerosing papillary lesion that in this location  is best classified as subareolar sclerosing papillary duct hyperplasia with  atypia  the issue of whether carcinoma has arisen in this tumor is difficult  to resolve  here  as is all too often the case today  we are asked to make a  diagnosis in a lesion that has been substantially distorted by an antecedent  core biopsy  reactive changes and epithelial displacement due to the earlier  biopsy are clearly present  i agree that there are some foci where expansile  growth at the perimeter appears to be intrinsic to the lesion and not  necessarily attributable to the earlier procedure  loss of myoepithelium may  occur focally in sclerosing benign papillary lesions but in my experience this  tends to occur more prominantly centrally rather than at the periphery as seen  in your case   it is possible for carcinoma to arise in a subareolar sclerosing duct  hyperplasia as described in breast pathology  2nd edition  p  95  authored by  dr  rosen  and i am concerned it may be present in this case  to investigate  this further i hope you will indulge me by providing six  6  superficial  unstained recuts each from blocks  1    2   and  3  on slides suitable for  immunostains    unstained slides were sent to dr  rosen     removed_accession_id  immunostaining on sections from blocks  1  to  3  show strong reactivity in  the center of the lesion with attenuation and focal loss of reactivity at the  periphery  basement membrane is retained throughout the entire lesion as  demonstrated by laminin and or collagen 4 reactivity  the h e appearance of  the hyperplastic epithelium in peripheral regions where myoepithelium is  depleted does not differ appreciably from central regions where it is present   the results are perplexing  not having done these studies previously in a case  of subareolar sclerosing papillary duct hyperplasia i am inclined to be  guided by the h e appearance of the lesion  which i interpret as atypical  hyperplasia   clinical data   history  0 8cm retroareolar mass found on screening mammography  a core  needle biopsy revealed only normal tissue   clinical diagnosis  left breast mass   radiology report  mass in specimen  dr  l  twohig  tissue submitted  1  left needle localization breast biopsy   gross description  by kwaan mary r  m d   the specimen is received fresh   labeled remove_patient_name  patient s name and unit  number  with no marking sutures and is a removed_date x removed_date x 1 1cm portion of breast  tissue with a wire protruding into the specimen  an accompanying specimen  radiograph reveals a central density with slightly irregular margins and the  localization wire   the specimen is inked black  there is a removed_date x removed_date x 0 9cm white firm mass  with slightly irregular borders  the mass is 0 1cm from the nearest resection  margin   micro removed_date  sections through long axis of the mass  1 frag each  rss   micro 3  sections showing closest margin and remainder of mass  3 frags   ess   micro removed_date  remainder of specimen  5 frags  ess   note  the entire specimen is submitted for microscopic examination   reports to  reilly  raymond  john  m b b c h   specimen type  breast   excisional tissue w margins   report_end  , Absent.
